<section class="net-reading" aria-labelledby="net-about-exam">
  <h2 id="net-about-exam">What is UGC-NET?</h2>
  <p>The University Grants Commission National Eligibility Test is conducted by the National Testing Agency (NTA). It is used for Junior Research Fellowship (JRF), Assistant Professor eligibility and Ph.D. admission. What a qualifying result allows depends on the candidate’s qualification category.</p>
  <p>For English Literature, choose <strong>English — subject code 30</strong>. Paper 1 is common to candidates across subjects; Paper 2 tests knowledge of the chosen subject.</p>
  <div class="net-detail-links"><a href="/exams/net/syllabus/">Explore the NET syllabus<img src="/assets/icons/feather/arrow-right.svg" alt="" width="20" height="20"></a></div>
</section>

<section class="net-reading" aria-labelledby="net-about-pattern">
  <h2 id="net-about-pattern">Exam pattern and marking</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <caption>Both papers are taken in one computer-based sitting.</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Paper</th><th scope="col">Content</th><th scope="col">Questions</th><th scope="col">Marks</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Paper 1</th><td>Teaching and research aptitude</td><td>50</td><td>100</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Paper 2</th><td>English Literature</td><td>100</td><td>200</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Total</th><td>3 hours, with no break between papers</td><td>150</td><td>300</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <ul>
    <li>Questions are objective multiple-choice questions.</li>
    <li>Each correct answer earns <strong>2 marks</strong>.</li>
    <li>There is <strong>no negative marking</strong> for an incorrect answer.</li>
    <li>The two papers share a single <strong>180-minute</strong> session.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="net-reading" aria-labelledby="net-about-eligibility">
  <h2 id="net-about-eligibility">Who can apply?</h2>
  <p>For the Master’s-degree route, General/Unreserved and General-EWS candidates normally need at least <strong>55%</strong>. The requirement is <strong>50%</strong> for the OBC-NCL, SC, ST, PwD/PwBD and Third gender categories specified in the bulletin. Candidates awaiting their qualifying result may apply provisionally, subject to NTA’s completion conditions.</p>
  <p>A separate four-year bachelor’s-degree route exists for JRF and Ph.D. admission, with its own marks requirements; it does not give Assistant Professor eligibility. Check the current bulletin for your route, age limits, relaxations and category conditions.</p>
  <p class="net-source-note">Exam facts checked against the NTA December 2026 information bulletin, updated 16 October 2026. <a href="https://ugcnet.nta.nic.in/information-bulletin/" rel="noopener">Read the official NTA information bulletin</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="net-reading" aria-labelledby="net-about-preparation">
  <h2 id="net-about-preparation">How to work through the subject</h2>
  <p>Use the syllabus as a checklist, and give reading, practice and revision their own place in your plan.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Start with chronology.</strong> Read the history of English literature age by age, from Old English to the present. A timeline helps you place writers, works and movements.</li>
    <li><strong>Make room for criticism and theory early.</strong> Return to these topics in short, regular revision sessions.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep Paper 1 in your plan.</strong> Work on teaching, research, reasoning and comprehension alongside your English reading.</li>
    <li><strong>Use previous-year questions to diagnose gaps.</strong> Separate an unfamiliar fact from a misread question, then revise accordingly.</li>
    <li><strong>Revise in cycles.</strong> Keep condensed notes and return to them instead of treating a unit as finished after one reading.</li>
  </ol>
